.TH OVCP 8 "2026-10-16" "ovcp" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
ovcp \- OpenVPN Control Plane: web UI, native PKI, and lifecycle management for an OpenVPN server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ovcp
[\fB\-data\fR \fIDIR\fR]
[\fB\-no\-color\fR]
[\fB\-json\fR]
[\fB\-debug\fR]
\fICOMMAND\fR
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ovcp
is a single static binary that manages one OpenVPN server: a built\-in
certificate authority (no easy\-rsa), client profile export, server
configuration, and a web admin UI. It talks to the running
.BR openvpn (8)
process over its management \fIunix socket\fR to disconnect clients (the
connected\-client list comes from openvpn's periodic status file, see
\fBFILES\fR) and manages everything else through files in its data
directory. It starts openvpn as a supervised foreground child and runs
unprivileged, identically on a host, under systemd and in a container (see
\fBPRIVILEGES\fR). Commands that use the data directory run as root or as
the \fIovcp\fR account; the examples on this page omit \fBsudo\fR.
.PP
The CA private key is encrypted at rest; a passphrase held only in memory
gates every signing operation (see \fBSECURITY\fR).
.PP
This page is also rendered into the web UI's \fBDocs\fR tab at build time.
.PP
This page is the complete runtime reference. Building and packaging are
documented in \fBBUILD.md\fR; internal design in \fBARCHITECTURE.md\fR.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.BR openvpn (8)
on \fBPATH\fR \(en ovcp starts, stops, and controls it but never bundles
it. Linux with \fI/dev/net/tun\fR; root or \fBCAP_NET_ADMIN\fR for openvpn
to bring the tunnel up (see \fBPRIVILEGES\fR).
.BR dnsmasq (8)
on \fBPATH\fR is optional, for the
.B dns
feature (internal DNS: resolve the server and connected clients by
hostname over the tunnel) \(en ovcp starts and supervises it the same way
as openvpn, but never installs it. \fBnft\fR(8) on \fBPATH\fR is also
optional, for the \fBupstream\fR feature (policy\-routed VPN egress) \(en
ovcp programs routing/NAT itself, but never installs nftables. Neither
missing dependency blocks
.B ovcp
itself from running \(en the feature that needs it is just unavailable
until it's on \fBPATH\fR (see
.B dns status
/
.B upstream status
in \fBCOMMANDS\fR).
.PP
Building ovcp itself \(en compiler and toolchain requirements,
cross\-compiling, packaging, and the container image \(en is documented in
\fBBUILD.md\fR, not here.
.SH SECURITY
Tier\-2 CA model: the CA private key is encrypted at rest (argon2id +
AES\-256\-GCM); every signing operation (\fBcertificate issue\fR,
\fBcertificate revoke\fR) requires
the operator passphrase, used in memory and never persisted.
\fBca rotate\fR re\-encrypts it under a new passphrase without reissuing
anything; \fBca renew\fR is the opposite axis \(en it reissues the CA
certificate itself (new serial, extended validity) under the unchanged key,
so every already\-issued cert stays valid without reissuing.
.PP
Every operator secret \(en the CA passphrase, admin/user passwords \(en
must be at least 8 characters and mix at least 2 of upper/lower/digit/
symbol, enforced the same way whether set interactively, via
\fBOVCP_CA_PASSPHRASE\fR / \fBOVCP_USER_PASSWORD\fR, or through the web UI.
\fBOVCP_YOLO_PASSWORD\fR (any non\-empty value) waives this requirement in
the CLI and, through \fBserve\fR's environment, in the web UI. Every weak
secret accepted this way is audited as \fBweak_password_accepted\fR.
.PP
Client private keys are embedded in the exported profile once and never
stored server\-side (no escrow): a lost profile means revoke + reissue, not
recovery. Certificates (public) are kept and downloadable anytime. Optionally
protect a profile's embedded key with its own password
(\fB\-key\-pass\fR / the web UI's field); OpenVPN prompts for it on connect.
.PP
The management socket is unix\-only, never TCP. The admin UI is HTTPS\-only
(self\-signed certificate, regenerated automatically if it expires or the
CN changes), loopback by default, with signed sessions (HttpOnly, Secure,
SameSite=Strict cookies), CSRF tokens on every mutating request, RBAC
(\fBadmin\fR / \fBoperator\fR / \fBreadonly\fR, see \fBWEB API\fR), optional
TOTP 2FA, rate\-limited logins, and a full audit log (\fBaudit\fR). Failed
logins lock out a username+IP pair after 5 attempts within 15 minutes,
self\-clearing once the window passes; a separate, looser limit locks out
the username alone after 20 attempts in the same window, to catch a brute
force spread across many source IPs. A password change (\fBuser passwd\fR
or the web UI) ends every session of that user, and a deleted or disabled
user's sessions stop working at once.
.PP
\fBvpn config set \-extra\fR accepts only an allowlist of directives (no
scripts, plugins or file loading; see \fBvpn config set\fR), so server
config can't run code as the \fIovcp\fR account. \fBcertificate issue
\-export\fR's \fB\-custom\-opts\fR appends raw directives to one client's
profile: that profile runs on the client's machine, and a bad line breaks
only that client.
.PP
The Telegram bot token (see \fBtelegram\fR) is the one secret stored in
plaintext, not hashed or encrypted \(en unlike the CA passphrase, \fBovcp\fR
needs the raw token on every restart to call Telegram's API, so it can't
be argon2id\-hashed the way a password is. Same exposure tier as
\fBOVCP_CA_PASSPHRASE\fR/\fBOVCP_USER_PASSWORD\fR: a leaked token lets
someone send/read messages as that bot, nothing more \(en it grants no
access to the CA, the database, or the admin UI. Every inbound message is
checked against exactly one configured Telegram identity (numeric id or
\fB@username\fR); every other sender is ignored: no reply, only a debug
log line. The bot can only report status and start, stop, restart or
reconnect the workers; certificate, user, CA, configuration and backup
operations are unreachable from Telegram (see \fBtelegram\fR in
\fBCOMMANDS\fR). Its lifecycle buttons use the same overlap guard as
\fBvpn\fR and \fBdns\fR.
.SH COMMANDS
A client or server certificate CN, a username and an upstream ID are each 1
to 64 (RFC 5280's CommonName limit) of \fBA\-Z a\-z 0\-9 . _ @ \-\fR, so a
name is safe verbatim in OpenVPN's management commands, configs, hosts
files, logs and tables. Further conventions every command shares:
.IP \(bu 2
A \fBset\fR flag left out keeps the current value. Booleans take
\fBon\fR/\fBoff\fR, \fBtrue\fR/\fBfalse\fR or \fB1\fR/\fB0\fR,
case\-insensitive.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-extra\fR and \fB\-custom\-opts\fR take a list of directive lines in
three forms: one line per use, repeatable and verbatim (commas included); a
bare \fB\-\fR, which opens the current lines in \fB$EDITOR\fR (default
\fBvi\fR, run by the shell, so it may carry arguments such as \fBcode
\-\-wait\fR); or a file by way of the shell, \fB"$(cat FILE)"\fR. Any use
replaces the whole list; \fB""\fR clears it.
.IP \(bu 2
An \fBOVCP_*\fR variable that seeds a setting (see \fBENVIRONMENT\fR) only
applies until that setting is first saved; after that, the command
changes it.
.IP \(bu 2
openvpn reloads nothing in place: a server config, server certificate or
CA certificate change takes effect on the next \fBvpn restart\fR. A
revocation needs none; openvpn reads the CRL on every connection.
.IP \(bu 2
Every mutation also has a web API route with the same implementation (see
\fBWEB API\fR); the web UI is built on those routes.
.TP
.B init \fB\-server\-cn\fR \fIHOST\fR [\fB\-ca\-cn\fR \fINAME\fR] [\fB\-ca\-years\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-server\-days\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-admin\fR \fINAME\fR] [\fB\-mgmt\fR \fIPATH\fR]
One\-shot initialization. Creates the CA (prompting twice for a new
passphrase), issues the server certificate for \fIHOST\fR, generates the
tls\-crypt key, writes \fIserver.conf\fR, and creates the initial admin
user. Idempotent: existing artifacts are kept. Pass \fB\-admin\fR "" to
skip user creation.
.TP
.B init \fB\-skeleton\fR
Creates or repairs \fI$OVCP_DATA\fR's directory tree and permissions only,
skipping the CA/admin/server\-cert steps above entirely \(en safe to run
anytime, and what packaging scripts use instead of their own
\fBmkdir\fR/\fBchown\fR/\fBchmod\fR (see \fBPRIVILEGES\fR).
.TP
.B serve [\fB\-listen\fR \fIADDR\fR] [\fB\-mgmt\fR \fIPATH\fR] [\fB\-ctrl\fR \fIPATH\fR]
Run the HTTPS admin UI and web API (default \fI127.0.0.1:8443\fR, self\-signed
certificate generated on first run). \fB\-mgmt\fR and \fB\-ctrl\fR override
\fBOVCP_MGMT_SOCK\fR and \fBOVCP_CTRL_SOCK\fR for this invocation. Starts the
.B openvpn
worker from \fIserver.conf\fR as a foreground child (reaped on exit \(en no
zombies) with its log in \fIlogs/openvpn.log\fR, and stops it on clean shutdown.
An exit that
.B ovcp
did not itself request (crash, OOM\-kill, an external \fBkill \-9\fR) is
logged at error level and auto\-restarted after 3 seconds; a config bad
enough to make openvpn exit repeatedly trips a breaker after 5 attempts
within 60 seconds, logging an error instead of retrying forever (fix the
config, then \fBovcp vpn start\fR). A deliberate
.B ovcp vpn stop
never auto\-restarts.
Exposes an owner\-only control socket so a separate
.B ovcp vpn
invocation can drive the worker. Refuses to start with an
actionable error if
.B ovcp init
has not been run.
.TP
.B certificate issue \fB\-cn\fR \fINAME\fR [\fB\-renew\fR] [\fB\-days\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-key\-pass\fR \fIPW\fR]
.TQ
.B certificate issue \fB\-cn\fR \fINAME\fR \fB\-export\fR [\fB\-renew\fR] [\fB\-remote\fR \fIHOST\fR] [\fB\-port\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-proto\fR udp|tcp] [\fB\-server\-cn\fR \fICN\fR] [\fB\-key\-pass\fR \fIPW\fR] [\fB\-split\-tunnel\fR] [\fB\-custom\-opts\fR \fIDIRECTIVE\fR ...|\fB\-\fR]
\fBcert\fR is accepted everywhere as a shorthand for \fBcertificate\fR
(\fBovcp cert list\fR, \fBovcp cert issue ...\fR); the rest of this page
spells it out in full. Client\-only \(en a server has exactly one cert,
tied to the file paths \fBserve\fR reads, and only \fBinit\fR (the first
one) and \fBcertificate renew\fR (every one after) ever write those files,
so \fBissue\fR never offers a server option that would just add a
disconnected DB row.
.PP
Covers the whole client lifecycle. Without \fB\-renew\fR, an active CN is
refused (one certificate per CN may be active at a time); with
\fB\-renew\fR, the outgoing cert is revoked and the CRL regenerated as
part of the same call \(en explicit rather than auto\-detected, so a
typo'd CN fails fast instead of silently replacing a live client's cert.
\fB\-export\fR switches the output from raw cert+key to a complete inline
\fI.ovpn\fR profile (CA, cert, key, tls\-crypt); combine both to renew a
client and hand back a fresh profile in one call. Output goes to stdout
only; private keys are printed exactly once and never stored (if lost,
\fB\-renew\fR). Save key\-bearing output with a private umask, e.g.
\fB(umask 077; ovcp certificate issue \-cn alice \-export > alice.ovpn)\fR. Certificates (public) are kept and can be re\-downloaded
anytime with \fBcertificate list\fR / the web UI. With \fB\-key\-pass\fR
the private key is AES\-256 encrypted; OpenVPN prompts for the password on
connect. \fB\-days\fR must be positive.
.RS
.TP
\fB\-export\fR's own flags
\fB\-remote\fR defaults to \fBOVCP_SERVER_CN\fR / the server cert's CN, and
is only required if neither is set. \fB\-port\fR, \fB\-proto\fR, and the
data cipher default to the server's configured values (see \fBvpn config
get\fR / \fBvpn config set\fR, or the web UI's Settings tab \(en both stay
in sync); pass \fB\-port\fR or \fB\-proto\fR to override just this one
export.
.TP
\fB\-split\-tunnel\fR
Adds a client\-side \fBpull\-filter\fR that ignores the server's pushed
\fBredirect\-gateway\fR, so this one client keeps its own default route
instead of tunneling all traffic; every other pushed option (DNS, explicit
routes) still applies. Only accepted when the server itself redirects all
traffic (\fBvpn config set \-redirect\-gw\fR) \(en rejected otherwise,
before anything is issued, since there is nothing to opt out of.
.TP
\fB\-custom\-opts\fR
Appends raw openvpn directives to this one client's profile, unvalidated
\(en a bad line only breaks that one client's connection, never the
server. Takes the list forms described at the top of \fBCOMMANDS\fR, e.g.
\fB\-custom\-opts\fR "keepalive 5 30" \fB\-custom\-opts\fR "verb 4".
.RE
.TP
.B certificate list [\fB\-status\fR all|active|revoked] [\fB\-kind\fR all|client|server] [\fB\-sort\fR cn|kind|expiry|serial|status] [\fB\-desc\fR]
List issued certificates with status (valid, expiring, REVOKED, expired;
expiring is anything within 30 days of \fBNotAfter\fR, matching the web
UI's Certs tab), colored accordingly (see \fB\-no\-color\fR).
\fB\-status\fR/\fB\-kind\fR filter before
printing (default \fBall\fR). \fB\-sort\fR reorders the
filtered list (default: issued order); \fB\-desc\fR reverses it. Same
filters/sort as the web UI's Certs tab. \fB\-json\fR prints the same rows
as JSON instead (see \fBMACHINE\-READABLE OUTPUT\fR). Never includes the
CA \(en see \fBca status\fR.
.TP
.B certificate revoke \fB\-serial\fR \fIHEX\fR
Revoke a client certificate and regenerate the CRL, then restart the CN's live
sessions (OpenVPN's RESTART, through a running \fBserve\fR) so their
reconnect is refused by \fBcrl\-verify\fR, which is always configured. The
CA passphrase is checked before anything changes.
\fBissue \-renew\fR does the same for the outgoing cert: clients already
on the new profile reconnect normally. The server cert is never revoked:
\fBcertificate renew\fR replaces it.
.TP
.B certificate renew [\fB\-cn\fR \fINAME\fR] [\fB\-days\fR \fIN\fR]
Server\-only \(en a server has exactly one cert, at the fixed paths
.B serve
reads directly, so nothing is printed (see \fBcertificate issue\fR above). Reissues it in place \(en
fresh keypair and serial, same paths. Omit \fB\-cn\fR to keep
the current CN (routine renewal); pass a different one to rename it \(en
server identity is kind\-only, not CN\-keyed like a client's (there is
only ever one), so renew and rename are the same operation. A rename
takes effect immediately everywhere else (DNS, the web UI's own title,
TOTP enrollment, client exports, log filenames \(en all read the CN
live, never cached), except the admin UI's own TLS cert, which keeps
the old CN until \fBovcp serve\fR itself is restarted (see
\fBadmin\-tls/\fR below). \fB\-cn\fR
defaults to the existing server cert's CN, falling back to
\fBOVCP_SERVER_CN\fR if that file can't be read yet (e.g. right after a
\fBbackup restore\fR, which never restores a server cert \(en see below).
.TP
.B vpn config get
Print the persisted server configuration \(en proto, port, subnet,
cipher, pushed DNS/routes, push\-internal\-dns, redirect\-gateway, and the
extra directives.
.TP
.B vpn config set [\fB\-proto\fR udp|tcp] [\fB\-port\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-subnet\fR \fICIDR\fR] [\fB\-cipher\fR \fINAME\fR] [\fB\-dns\fR \fIIPS\fR] [\fB\-push\-internal\-dns\fR on|off] [\fB\-routes\fR \fICIDRS\fR] [\fB\-redirect\-gw\fR on|off] [\fB\-extra\fR \fIDIRECTIVE\fR ...|\fB\-\fR]
Change one or more server settings and persist them, printing the same
\fB+\fR/\fB\-\fR diff \fBpreview\fR below shows. \fB\-dns\fR and
\fB\-routes\fR take a comma\-separated list (\fB\-dns\fR "1.1.1.1, 9.9.9.9"),
replacing the whole list, not appending to it. Each \fB\-extra\fR line must
name an allowed directive (comments and
blank lines aside): \fBlocal\fR, \fBpush\fR, \fBkeepalive\fR (overrides the
built\-in \fIkeepalive 10 60\fR), \fBclient\-to\-client\fR, \fBmax\-clients\fR,
\fBmax\-routes\-per\-client\fR, \fBinactive\fR, \fBsession\-timeout\fR,
\fBconnect\-freq\fR, \fBhand\-window\fR, \fBtls\-version\-min\fR/\fB\-max\fR,
\fBtls\-cert\-profile\fR, \fBtls\-cipher\fR, \fBtls\-ciphersuites\fR,
\fBecdh\-curve\fR, \fBremote\-cert\-eku\fR/\fB\-ku\fR, \fBreneg\-bytes\fR/\fB\-pkts\fR/\fB\-sec\fR,
\fBreplay\-window\fR, \fBmute\-replay\-warnings\fR, \fBmute\fR, \fBmssfix\fR,
\fBfragment\fR, \fBtun\-mtu\fR, \fBtcp\-nodelay\fR. Scripts, plugins, file
loading, inline blocks and what \fBovcp\fR renders itself are refused, from
\fBOVCP_OPENVPN_EXTRA\fR too. Rejects an invalid combination (bad CIDR,
disallowed cipher or extra directive, port out of range, an unrecognized
boolean) without writing anything; an empty diff \(en including an
\fB$EDITOR\fR session that saved unmodified \(en is reported, never
written or audited.
.RS
.TP
\fBPush internal DNS\fR
\fB\-push\-internal\-dns\fR (off by default; \fBovcp init\fR turns it on
automatically when it finds dnsmasq on \fBPATH\fR) pushes the server's own
dnsmasq resolver \(en its VPN\-side tun IP, e.g. \fI10.8.0.1\fR \(en to
clients first, ahead of anything in \fB\-dns\fR. That ordering is what lets
internal cert\-hostnames (see \fBrecord\fR below, \fIcerts.hosts\fR /
\fIcustom.hosts\fR) resolve at all: without it, a client only ever asks
whatever DNS it already had, and never learns those names exist. When the
server CN is a hostname (\fIvpn.example.com\fR), its parent domain
(\fIexample.com\fR) is pushed as the search domain and also names each
client in \fIcerts.hosts\fR (\fIalice.example.com\fR); an IP\-address CN
adds neither. It
interacts directly with \fBdns config set \-block\-internet\fR below: that
setting makes dnsmasq itself refuse to forward upstream, but it's no
protection at all for a client that was never told to use dnsmasq as its
resolver in the first place \(en \fB\-push\-internal\-dns\fR is what makes
that client send queries there. Likewise, manual entries left in
\fB\-dns\fR alongside \fB\-block\-internet on\fR let a client route around
the block by falling through to one of those other pushed resolvers.
\fBvpn config set\fR and \fBdns config set\fR both print a non\-fatal
warning (CLI: stderr; web UI: inline) when they detect either combination,
but never block the save \(en it's occasionally the intended setup (e.g. a
resolver kept reachable for split\-DNS).
.RE
.TP
.B vpn config preview [\fIsame flags as\fR \fBvpn config set\fR]
Show what \fBvpn config set\fR with the same flags would change in
\fIserver.conf\fR (unified \fB+\fR/\fB\-\fR diff lines), without persisting
or writing anything. Safe to run anytime: nothing is
saved until the same flags are run through \fBvpn config set\fR instead \(en
\fBvpn config get\fR right after a \fBpreview\fR still shows the old values.
.TP
.B ca rotate
Re\-encrypt the CA private key under a new passphrase (prompts for the
current one, then the new one twice). The CA keypair and certificate are
unchanged \(en issued certificates stay valid, nothing needs reissuing.
\fICLI\-only\fR, so the new passphrase never crosses HTTP.
.TP
.B ca renew [\fB\-years\fR \fIN\fR]
Reissue the CA certificate itself in place: a fresh serial and extended
validity (default 10 years), signed with the unchanged CA key. Distinct
from \fBca rotate\fR (passphrase only) \(en this is what to run if the CA
certificate's own expiry, not its passphrase, is the problem. Every
already\-issued client/server cert stays valid: verification only checks
the CA's signing key, never which CA certificate happens to be on disk, so
nothing needs reissuing. \fICLI\-only\fR, like \fBca rotate\fR: a mistake or a hijacked admin session
on the CA affects every certificate, so CA operations require shell access.
The web UI shows the CA status (Dashboard) only.
.TP
.B ca status
Read\-only: CN, expiry, and status (valid, expiring, expired \(en the CA
can't be revoked, so never that). Kept out of \fBcertificate list\fR on
purpose \(en the CA lives on its own path, CLI/admin\-only, never mixed
into the client/server certs surface.
.TP
.B backup create
Write an encrypted archive (argon2id + AES\-256\-GCM, prompts twice for a
backup passphrase) of the CA, CRL, tls\-crypt key, TOTP key,
\fIserver.conf\fR, and database to stdout: \fBovcp backup create >
ovcp.bak\fR. Refuses a terminal stdout and \fB\-json\fR (the archive is
the output). Excludes the openvpn server certificate and private key:
OpenVPN clients trust the CA chain and a CN match, never a pinned server
cert, so a restore just issues a fresh one (\fBcertificate renew\fR)
\(en the server's private key never has to leave the
machine. Client private keys
were never stored here to begin with (no escrow); nothing to back up there
either.
.TP
.B backup restore [\fB\-force\fR] < \fIARCHIVE\fR
Decrypt and extract the archive read from stdin into \fB\-data\fR (must be
an uninitialized directory, or pass \fB\-force\fR to overwrite one):
\fBovcp backup restore < ovcp.bak\fR. Refuses a terminal stdin. \fICLI\-only\fR: restoring is an offline
disaster\-recovery operation on a data directory, not exposed over HTTP. Follow with \fBcertificate renew\fR (the archive has
no server cert) and \fBovcp vpn start\fR.
.TP
.B status [\fB\-ctrl\fR \fIPATH\fR]
Full\-system overview: whether the openvpn worker is running (pid, connected
clients, via the control socket, see \fBOVCP_CTRL_SOCK\fR), cert counts
(total/expiring, same classification as \fBcertificate list\fR), and the
Telegram bot's state \(en everything \fBovcp vpn|dns|telegram status\fR and
\fBcertificate list\fR each report individually, in one place. Traffic
history and per\-client sessions are \fBstats\fR's job, not this command's.
\fBserve\fR unreachable reports openvpn as \fIunknown\fR (not \fIstopped\fR)
with the connection error, and dns/Telegram \fIunknown\fR too \(en same
socket, so a real answer for one but not the others isn't possible. Cert
counts are unaffected, read straight from the database either way.
.TP
.B stats [\fB\-cn\fR \fINAME\fR] [\fB\-follow\fR] [\fB\-interval\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-ctrl\fR \fIPATH\fR]
.TQ
.B stats config get
.TQ
.B stats config set [\fB\-sample\-interval\-sec\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-retention\-hours\fR \fIN\fR]
Traffic history: recent throughput/volume and finished client sessions,
globally or for one client (\fB\-cn\fR), read straight from the database
\(en works even with \fBserve\fR not running. Same data as the web UI's
Stats tab. \fB\-follow\fR switches to a live top\-like view instead: polls
a running \fBserve\fR's control socket (not the database) every
\fB\-interval\fR seconds (default 2) and redraws in
place, sorted busiest\-first when global; rejects \fB\-json\fR (a live
view, not a single result) and needs \fBserve\fR running.
.B stats config get\fR/\fBstats config set
manage the sampler that feeds all of the above, independently of
\fB\-follow\fR/\fB\-interval\fR (which only control this one invocation's
polling, not the sampler itself): \fB\-sample\-interval\-sec\fR (default
60) is how often a sample is written per connected client;
\fB\-retention\-hours\fR (default 24) is how far back samples/sessions are
kept and queried. Both must be positive. The running sampler picks them up
on its next tick, no restart needed.
.TP
.B vpn start|stop|restart|reconnect|status [\fB\-ctrl\fR \fIPATH\fR]
.TQ
.B vpn kill \fB\-cn\fR \fINAME\fR [\fB\-ctrl\fR \fIPATH\fR]
Manage or inspect the openvpn worker owned by a running
.B serve
(over the control socket, see \fBOVCP_CTRL_SOCK\fR).
.B start
launches it (idempotent),
.B stop
sends SIGTERM,
.B status
reports whether it is running,
.B restart
is a full stop + fresh start, what any config or certificate change needs;
\fBstart\fR/\fBrestart\fR also re\-apply the upstream
configuration (see \fBupstream\fR below). And
.B reconnect
sends SIGUSR1 for a soft session reset (runtime recovery, no process
replacement).
.B kill
tells every session of a CN to stop (OpenVPN's HALT): the client exits
instead of reconnecting, and the worker process is untouched. SIGHUP / in\-place reload is intentionally not
supported: a fresh restart re\-reads every file, which SIGHUP
cannot. Each lifecycle op prints the resulting openvpn pid, so a repeated
.B start
reports \(lqalready started\(rq rather than spawning a second process.
.BR start / stop / restart / reconnect
reject instead of queuing behind one already in flight for the same
worker (\(lqoperation already in progress\(rq) \(en a second call has to
wait for the first to actually finish, not just get delayed behind it;
a scripted loop calling
.B restart
faster than openvpn itself restarts gets mostly rejections, not a stack
of real restarts. \fBdns\fR's lifecycle ops and \fBtelegram\fR's
\fBstart\fR/\fBstop\fR/\fBrestart\fR share the same guard, independently
per worker.
.TP
.B dns start|stop|restart|status [\fB\-ctrl\fR \fIPATH\fR]
Manage or inspect the dnsmasq worker the same way
.B vpn
manages openvpn. No
.B reconnect
(dnsmasq has no such signal). If dnsmasq isn't installed, every op
fails with a clear error.
.TP
.B dns config get
.TQ
.B dns config set [\fB\-block\-internet\fR on|off]
Whether dnsmasq also forwards to upstream resolvers, or answers only
names it knows locally \(en the same persisted setting as the Records
tab's \(lqBlock internet DNS\(rq toggle. Unlike
.BR start / stop / restart / status
above, this is a stored setting, not a live control\-socket op \(en it
works without a running
.B serve
and takes effect on dnsmasq's next
.B start
or
.BR restart .
Default off.
.B dns config get
prints the current value; omitting \fB\-block\-internet\fR makes \fBset\fR
a no\-op. See
\fBPush internal DNS\fR under \fBvpn config set\fR above for how this
interacts with that setting:
turning this on without also pushing the internal resolver, or combining
it with manually pushed DNS servers (\fBvpn config set \-dns\fR), leaves a
documented leak that both commands warn about on save (non\-fatal).
.TP
.B record list [\fB\-status\fR all|active|revoked] [\fB\-kind\fR all|client|server] [\fB\-sort\fR cn|kind|expiry|serial|status] [\fB\-desc\fR]
.TQ
.B record edit
.TQ
.B record include|exclude \fB\-cn\fR \fINAME\fR
Manage internal DNS records (see \fBFILES\fR, \fIcerts.hosts\fR and
\fIcustom.hosts\fR).
.B list
prints every certificate (not just currently-connected ones) with its DNS
include/exclude status, same filters/sort/coloring as \fBcertificate
list\fR, plus \fIcustom.hosts\fR' raw content.
.B edit
opens the custom entries in \fB$EDITOR\fR (default \fBvi\fR).
.B include
and
.B exclude
control whether a client certificate resolves while connected (default:
included), mirroring \fBuser enable|disable\fR. Client\-only \(en the
server's own DNS entry is unconditional, so \fB\-cn\fR \fIserverCN\fR
errors the same as any CN with no active cert.
.TP
.B upstream list
.TQ
.B upstream add \fB\-id\fR \fINAME\fR (\fB\-iface\fR \fIIFACE\fR [\fB\-gateway\fR \fIIP\fR] | \fB\-socks\fR \fIURL\fR [\fB\-dial\-timeout\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-handshake\-timeout\fR \fIN\fR]) [\fB\-desc\fR \fITEXT\fR]
.TQ
.B upstream del \fB\-id\fR \fINAME\fR
.TQ
.B upstream set\-default (\fB\-id\fR \fINAME\fR | \fB\-clear\fR)
.TQ
.B upstream assign \fB\-cn\fR \fINAME\fR (\fB\-id\fR \fIID\fR | \fB\-clear\fR) [\fB\-ctrl\fR \fIPATH\fR]
.TQ
.B upstream reconcile [\fB\-ctrl\fR \fIPATH\fR]
.TQ
.B upstream status
Route VPN client traffic out an admin\-chosen egress instead of the box's
own default route: either
.B \-iface
(a second NIC, a WireGuard peer, anything else already up on the host \(en
routed at the IP layer, kill\-switched on its own if the gateway dies) or
.B \-socks
(a
.I socks5://[user:pass@]host:port
URL \(en Tor, proxychains, or any self\-hosted SOCKS5 backend, relayed
through a small in\-process proxy instead of routed). Exactly one of the two
is required; \fB\-gateway\fR only applies to \fB\-iface\fR mode.
.B ovcp
never brings the upstream connection itself up (nor the SOCKS backend) \(en
the admin does, by whatever means, and only names it here so
.B ovcp
knows where to steer matching traffic. A socks\-mode upstream has no
interface to route via: traffic is redirected locally to ovcp's own relay,
which speaks the SOCKS5 CONNECT handshake (user/pass auth if the URL carries
credentials) to the backend; it carries TCP only, never UDP, and fails
closed the same way \-iface's kill\-switch does \(en a dead or unreachable
backend just drops the connection, never falls through to the host's own
route. \fB\-dial\-timeout\fR/\fB\-handshake\-timeout\fR (seconds) override
this one upstream's connect/negotiation timeout \(en a fast local backend
wants these low, Tor wants headroom for real circuit\-build time; 0 (the
default) uses the server\-wide default (see \fBOVCP_UPSTREAM_SOCKS_DIAL_TIMEOUT\fR/
\fBOVCP_UPSTREAM_SOCKS_HANDSHAKE_TIMEOUT\fR in \fBENVIRONMENT\fR). The URL's
password never appears again in
.B list/reconcile/status
output or the web API once saved, only \fB***\fR.
.B add
assigns the next free routing table (or, for socks\-mode, the derived local
relay port) in ovcp's own reserved band automatically. \fBset\-default\fR is
the ad\-hoc exit switch \(en instant,
no
.B vpn restart
needed, since it only reprograms host routing/NAT, never openvpn itself;
\fB\-clear\fR (or an empty \fB\-id\fR) turns it off, back to the host's own
default route. \fBassign\fR overrides one client's (\fB\-cn\fR) egress
independent of the default \(en \fB\-clear\fR removes the override, falling
back to \fBset\-default\fR's choice instead. Takes effect instantly for an
already\-connected client too, not just on their next reconnect \(en
.B once
per\-CN routing is already active. The
.I first
\fB\-cn\fR assignment ever made flips on openvpn's
\fBmanagement\-client\-auth\fR, a config\-file\-only directive an already\-
running openvpn won't pick up on its own; \fBassign\fR prints a warning and
.B vpn restart
is required exactly that one time. Every rule this feature installs lives
inside a fixed,
ovcp\-owned band (routing tables, ip\-rule priorities, one nftables table)
so it can never collide with or remove a rule something else on the host
created; a dead gateway fails closed (a kill\-switch route inside its own
table) rather than falling through to the host's own default route.
An egress that isn't up yet (or goes down later, e.g. during maintenance)
never fails
.B add/del/set\-default
themselves \(en the config change still saves; a warning names which
upstream is currently unreachable. \fBreconcile\fR is the explicit
"situation's stable now" trigger \(en re\-applies the persisted config
(picking up anything that came back) and reports what's still down,
without needing a full \fBserve\fR restart. Not on any timer; admin\-
initiated only \(en \fBvpn start\fR/\fBrestart\fR trigger the same
re\-apply automatically, see \fBvpn\fR above. \fBupstream status\fR
reports nftables detection and the active default.
Every op fails cleanly if nftables isn't on \fBPATH\fR, or if
\fBOVCP_UPSTREAMS_ENABLED=false\fR (see \fBENVIRONMENT\fR) \(en same
treatment
.B dns
gets without dnsmasq; the web UI then hides the Upstreams tab.
.TP
.B logs config get
.TQ
.B logs config set [\fB\-enabled\fR on|off] [\fB\-retention\-days\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-keep\-uncompressed\fR \fIN\fR]
.TQ
.B logs rotate
.TQ
.B logs status
Manage rotation of \fIopenvpn.log\fR/\fIovcp.log\fR/\fIdnsmasq.log\fR (see
\fBFILES\fR): internal copytruncate, safe with the worker's already\-open
handle, no reopen or restart needed. Runs once a day by default while
\fBserve\fR is up.
.B logs config set \-enabled\fR
(default on) toggles that daily pass \(en turn it off to hand
rotation to an external \fBlogrotate\fR instead (see \fI/var/log/ovcp\fR in
\fBFILES\fR).
.B \-retention\-days
(default 14; \fB0\fR keeps forever) deletes rotated generations older than
that;
.B \-keep\-uncompressed
(default 2) gzips anything older than the newest N. Neither may be
negative.
.B rotate
runs one pass immediately \(en point an external cron at this if
\fB\-enabled\fR is off \(en and works whether or not \fBserve\fR is
running, same as \fBrecord\fR's CLI\-side refresh.
.B status
prints each source's current size, generation count, and oldest/newest
rotation timestamp.
.TP
.B user add \fB\-name\fR \fINAME\fR [\fB\-role\fR admin|operator|readonly]
.TQ
.B user list [\fB\-status\fR all|enabled|disabled] [\fB\-role\fR all|admin|operator|readonly] [\fB\-sort\fR username|role|created|status|2fa] [\fB\-desc\fR]
.TQ
.B user del|disable|enable|passwd \fB\-name\fR \fINAME\fR
.TQ
.B user totp \fB\-name\fR \fINAME\fR [\fB\-off\fR]
Manage web UI users; what each role may do is listed under \fBWEB API\fR
(\fBadd\fR defaults to \fBoperator\fR).
\fBuser list\fR's \fB\-status\fR/\fB\-role\fR filter before printing (default
\fBall\fR); \fB\-sort\fR reorders the filtered list (default: creation order);
\fB\-desc\fR reverses it, and its enabled/disabled status is colored (see
\fB\-no\-color\fR). Same as the web UI's Users tab \(en see
\fBMACHINE\-READABLE OUTPUT\fR for \fB\-json\fR's fields per subcommand.
.B user totp
prints an otpauth:// enrollment URL; \fB\-off\fR disables 2FA instead.
The web API refuses to delete or disable the caller's own account; the
CLI, run on the host, has no caller to protect and no such guard.
.TP
.B audit [\fB\-sort\fR ts|actor|action] [\fB\-desc\fR]
Print the last 50 audit entries (logins, issues, revocations, kills,
config changes), newest first by default; \fB\-sort\fR reorders them,
\fB\-desc\fR reverses it.
.TP
.B debug on|off [\fB\-ctrl\fR \fIPATH\fR]
Toggle verbose logging on a running \fBserve\fR (over the control socket,
see \fBOVCP_CTRL_SOCK\fR) \(en no restart, resets to normal on the next one.
.TP
.B telegram config get
.TQ
.B telegram config set [\fB\-token\fR] [\fB\-admin\fR \fIID\-or\-@user\fR]
.TQ
.B telegram start|stop|restart|status [\fB\-ctrl\fR \fIPATH\fR]
Notify\-only Telegram bot, with a narrow control surface. \fBtelegram
config set\fR sets the bot token and the one Telegram identity the bot
will ever respond to independently \(en pass either or both, at least one
required. \fB\-token\fR is a boolean flag, not a value (a secret has no
business on the command line): prompts interactively, or reads
\fBOVCP_TELEGRAM_TOKEN\fR, validated against Telegram's \fBgetMe\fR before
saving. \fB\-admin\fR takes the numeric user id or \fB@username\fR
directly (default \fBOVCP_TELEGRAM_ADMIN\fR if set); anyone else who
messages the bot is ignored (no reply).
\fBtelegram config get\fR prints whether a token is set, the current admin,
and whether the chat is linked yet (a \fB@username\fR admin only links once
they've messaged the bot; re\-saving the same token or admin never unlinks
it) \(en never the token itself. Changing \fB\-admin\fR to a different
identity best\-effort warns the chat being replaced first, naming the
incoming identity, before it's unlinked \(en the one defense against a
hijacked reassignment; changing \fB\-token\fR has no equivalent, an
already\-leaked token grants that access regardless.
\fBstart\fR/\fBstop\fR/\fBrestart\fR/\fBstatus\fR drive the poller inside a
running \fBserve\fR over the control socket (see \fBOVCP_CTRL_SOCK\fR),
same as \fBvpn\fR; the poller also auto\-starts with \fBserve\fR if a token
is already set. Once linked, the bot pushes notifications for revoke,
user add/del, CA rotate, CA renew, a cert (client, server, or the CA itself)
entering its 30\-day expiry window (once per cert; also logged at Warn in
\fIovcp.log\fR regardless of Telegram config), and openvpn/dnsmasq giving up
auto\-restart after crash\-looping;
\fB/status\fR, \fB/menu\fR (openvpn Start/Stop/Restart/Reconnect),
\fB/dns\fR (dnsmasq Start/Stop/Restart, same confirm step on Stop/Restart),
and \fB/certs\fR (every certificate \(en client, server, and the CA \(en
grouped by status: expiring, expired, revoked, then valid, most urgent
first, same \fBstore.Cert.Status()\fR classification \fBcertificate list\fR
and the web UI's Certificates table use) are the only commands it accepts
\(en revoke, user management, CA rotation/renewal, config, and backup stay
CLI/web\-login only; \fB/certs\fR is reporting only, with no renew or
revoke action reachable from it.
.TP
.B version
Print \fBovcp\fR's version, and the \fBopenvpn\fR and \fBdnsmasq\fR binaries'
versions and paths if found on \fBPATH\fR (advisory only \(en absent, not an
error, if not). Without dnsmasq, \fBdns\fR/\fBrecord\fR still run but fail
cleanly, and the web UI hides the Records tab.
.TP
.B completion bash|zsh|fish
Print a shell completion script to stdout. Install once, e.g.
\fBovcp completion bash > /etc/bash_completion.d/ovcp\fR; distro packages
already install these (see \fBBUILD.md\fR). Rejects \fB\-json\fR (see
\fBMACHINE\-READABLE OUTPUT\fR).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-data \fIDIR\fR
Data directory. Overrides \fB$OVCP_DATA\fR for this invocation; if neither
is given, defaults to \fI/var/lib/ovcp\fR. This directory is the root every
path in \fBFILES\fR below is relative to. Must come before the command
(\fBovcp \-data\fR \fIDIR\fR \fICOMMAND\fR), like \fBgit \-C\fR / \fBdocker
\-H\fR.
.TP
.B \-no\-color
Disable ANSI color: the status words in \fBcertificate list\fR/\fBrecord
list\fR/\fBstatus\fR/\fBvpn status\fR/\fBdns status\fR/\fBtelegram
status\fR/\fBuser list\fR/\fBca status\fR's text output, the \fB+\fR/\fB\-\fR
diff lines in \fBvpn config preview\fR, the platform/build tag in
\fBversion\fR, and the level tag in \fBovcp\fR's own log lines.
Same effect as \fB$NO_COLOR\fR. Color is already off automatically when the
relevant stream (stdout for table output, stderr for logs) isn't a
terminal \(en piped, journal, redirected to a file. Must come before the
command (\fBovcp \-no\-color\fR \fICOMMAND\fR), like \fB\-data\fR.
.TP
.B \-json
Machine\-readable JSON instead of human text, for whatever the command
produces. Same as \fB$OVCP_JSON\fR. For every command except \fBserve\fR,
this means its result on stdout (see \fBMACHINE\-READABLE OUTPUT\fR
below). For \fBserve\fR, which
has no single result, it means \fBovcp\fR's own log lines \(en both the
console/journal stream and the tee'd \fI$OVCP_DATA/logs/ovcp.log\fR \(en as
one JSON object per line (\fBtime\fR, \fBlevel\fR, \fBmsg\fR, plus whatever
attributes that log call carried) instead of \fBslog\fR's default
\fBkey=value\fR text, for log shippers/parsers. The web UI's Logs tab parses
either format. Must come before the command, like \fB\-data\fR (\fBovcp
\-json list\fR, \fBovcp \-json serve\fR).
.TP
.B \-debug
Debug\-level logging for this invocation only \(en every \fBslog.Debug\fR
call (HTTP request logging, telegram polling, ...) starts showing up
immediately, no restart or second command needed. For a running
\fBserve\fR, equivalent to sending \fBdebug on\fR over the control socket at
startup instead of afterwards (see \fBdebug\fR in \fBCOMMANDS\fR). Must come
before the command, like \fB\-data\fR.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print the command list (\fBCOMMANDS\fR above, condensed) and exit.
.SH MACHINE\-READABLE OUTPUT
\fB\-json\fR (or \fB$OVCP_JSON\fR) switches every command \(en not a
subset \(en from aligned human text to JSON on stdout. It changes only the
wire format: the same fields are computed either way, so nothing in text is
missing from JSON or vice versa, and the two modes are otherwise
interchangeable in scripts and by hand. Three commands reject \fB\-json\fR
because their output is not a result to encode: \fBstats \-follow\fR (a
live redrawing view), \fBcompletion\fR (a shell script for \fBeval\fR) and
\fBbackup create\fR (the archive itself). \fBserve\fR is the other special case: it never
exits with a single result, so \fB\-json\fR there means JSON log lines
instead (see \fB\-json\fR in \fBOPTIONS\fR).
.PP
Fatal errors are JSON too, on stdout, as \fB{"error": "..."}\fR \(en the
same shape every failed web API request uses. A script
can parse stdout unconditionally and check \fB.error\fR (or the process exit
code, still 1 on failure) rather than branching on success vs. failure
output. Flag\-parsing mistakes (an unknown or malformed flag) are the one
exception, always plain text on stderr from Go's own flag parser, before
\fBovcp\fR ever sees \fB\-json\fR.
.PP
\fB\-json\fR is a global flag: it comes before the command, like
\fB\-data\fR/\fB\-debug\fR/\fB\-no\-color\fR (\fBovcp \-json certificate
list\fR, not \fBovcp certificate list \-json\fR).
.PP
Fully unattended (Ansible, CI, any tool with no terminal to answer a
prompt): every passphrase/password/token \fBovcp\fR ever asks for also
reads from an env var (\fBOVCP_CA_PASSPHRASE\fR and friends, see
\fBENVIRONMENT\fR) \(en set it and the prompt never fires, on any command,
in either output mode. Combine with \fB\-json\fR/\fB$OVCP_JSON\fR for a
clean, scriptable round trip:
.RS
.nf
OVCP_JSON=1 OVCP_CA_PASSPHRASE=\(dq...\(dq ovcp certificate issue \-cn alice
.fi
.RE
Prompts are read from the controlling terminal, never stdin, so stdin and
stdout stay free for data (\fBbackup create\fR/\fBrestore\fR). No env var and
no controlling terminal fails fast with \fB{"error": "..."}\fR instead of
hanging on a prompt nobody can answer.
Per\-command JSON shapes:
.TP
.B init
\fB{caCert, serverCert, tlsCrypt, serverConf, admin, adminUIURL}\fR. The
\fB[N/5]\fR progress lines are human text only, suppressed under
\fB\-json\fR so they can't interleave with the result.
.TP
.B certificate issue
\fB{serial, cert, key}\fR, or with \fB\-export\fR \fB{serial, bundle}\fR
(the \fI.ovpn\fR bundle instead of cert/key), as JSON strings.
.TP
.B certificate revoke
\fB{serial, crlPath}\fR.
.TP
.B ca rotate, backup restore, user del,
.B user passwd, user totp \-off, record edit|include|exclude, init \-skeleton,
.B logs rotate, upstream add, upstream del, upstream set\-default,
.B upstream assign
\fB{message}\fR \(en a confirmation string, nothing structured beyond it.
Any apply\-time warning (e.g. a down egress iface) prints to stderr in both
output modes, same as \fB\-json\fR's \fB{error}\fR convention for failures
\(en never silently dropped, never smuggled into the JSON payload either.
.TP
.B certificate renew
\fB{cn, serial, notAfter}\fR \(en server\-only, nothing sensitive leaves
the machine.
.TP
.B ca renew
\fB{notAfter}\fR.
.TP
.B ca status
\fB{cn, notAfter, status}\fR.
.TP
.B certificate list
array of \fB{status, kind, cn, serial, expires}\fR.
.TP
.B vpn config get, vpn config set
The full persisted server config: \fB{Proto, Port, Subnet, Cipher, DNS,
PushInternalDNS, Routes, RedirectGW, Extra}\fR plus the \fBovcp\fR\-managed
path/runtime fields (\fBCACert, ServerCert, ServerKey, CRL, TLSCrypt,
MgmtSocket, StatusLog, StatusInterval, ServerCN\fR). Settings objects
(this one, \fBdns\fR, \fBlogs\fR and \fBstats config\fR) keep the Go field
names, capitalized.
.TP
.B vpn config preview
\fB{diff, changed}\fR: \fBdiff\fR an array of unified \fB+\fR/\fB\-\fR
\fIserver.conf\fR lines (empty if \fBchanged\fR is \fBfalse\fR).
.TP
.B dns config get, dns config set
\fB{BlockInternet}\fR.
.TP
.B logs config get, logs config set
\fB{Enabled, RetentionDays, KeepUncompressed}\fR.
.TP
.B logs status
array of \fB{Name, Path, CurrentSize, Generations, Oldest, Newest}\fR, one
row per log source (see \fBlogs\fR in \fBCOMMANDS\fR).
.TP
.B stats config get, stats config set
\fB{SampleIntervalSec, RetentionHours}\fR.
.TP
.B status
\fB{running, pid, uptime, ovcpUptime, error, clients, dnsRunning, dnsPid,
dnsUptime, certsTotal, certsExpiring, telegram}\fR \(en \fBpid\fR,
\fBuptime\fR, \fBovcpUptime\fR, \fBerror\fR, \fBdnsPid\fR, \fBdnsUptime\fR,
and \fBtelegram\fR are omitted, not sent as empty/zero, when not applicable
(e.g. \fBuptime\fR while stopped, \fBtelegram\fR if \fBserve\fR is
unreachable).
.TP
.B stats
\fB{samples, sessions}\fR (snapshot mode only \(en see \fB\-follow\fR
above).
.TP
.B vpn kill
\fB{message}\fR.
.TP
.B vpn (other ops), dns (excl. config), debug
\fBvpn\fR/\fBdns\fR lifecycle ops (\fBstart|stop|restart|reconnect|status\fR):
\fB{Pid, Changed, StartedAt, ServeStartedAt}\fR (untagged, like \fBvpn
config\fR above) for every op including \fBstatus\fR \(en \fBdns config\fR
is documented separately above. \fBdebug on\fR/\fBoff\fR: \fB{debug}\fR.
.TP
.B record list
\fB{certs, custom}\fR: \fBcerts\fR an array of \fB{status, kind, cn,
serial, expires, includeDNS, ip}\fR (same \fBstatus\fR values as
\fBcertificate list\fR; \fBip\fR omitted unless that CN is resolving
right now \(en connected, or the server's own always\-on address);
\fBcustom\fR is \fIcustom.hosts\fR' raw content as one string.
.TP
.B upstream list, upstream reconcile
\fB{upstreams: [{id, up, iface, gateway, socks, table, description}],
default, perCN}\fR \(en \fBiface\fR/\fBgateway\fR and \fBsocks\fR are
mutually exclusive per entry; \fBsocks\fR' password, if any, is always
\fB***\fR in this output, never the real value. \fBup\fR is a live
\fInet.InterfaceByName\fR check for \-iface mode, always true for \-socks
mode (no cheap liveness check without dialing the backend) \(en same value
the text table's STATUS column reads; \fBreconcile\fR re\-applies first.
.TP
.B upstream status
\fB{enabled, nftVersion, nftPath, default}\fR \(en version/path pair omitted
if nftables isn't on \fBPATH\fR.
.TP
.B telegram config get, telegram config set, telegram start|stop|restart|status
\fB{running, tokenSet, admin, chatLinked, changed}\fR \(en same shape from
every one of these, whether or not \fB\-token\fR/\fB\-admin\fR were passed
to \fBconfig set\fR. \fBchanged\fR is only meaningful for \fBstart\fR/
\fBstop\fR/\fBrestart\fR (whether the op actually flipped \fBrunning\fR,
same distinction \fBvpn\fR/\fBdns\fR's \fBChanged\fR makes above); always
\fBfalse\fR from \fBconfig get\fR, \fBconfig set\fR, and \fBstatus\fR.
.TP
.B user add
\fB{username, role}\fR.
.TP
.B user list
array of \fB{Username, Role, Disabled, TOTP, CreatedAt}\fR \(en never the
password hash or TOTP secret, in JSON any more than in text.
.TP
.B user disable|enable
\fB{username, disabled}\fR.
.TP
.B user totp
(enrollment) \fB{secret, url}\fR \(en the QR code itself is terminal art,
skipped entirely under \fB\-json\fR.
.TP
.B audit
array of the same entries as text.
.TP
.B version
\fB{version, platform, buildTarget, openvpnVersion, openvpnPath,
dnsmasqVersion, dnsmasqPath, nftVersion, nftPath}\fR \(en each pair omitted
if that binary isn't on \fBPATH\fR (see \fBversion\fR in \fBCOMMANDS\fR).
.SH WEB API
\fBserve\fR's HTTPS listener serves the web UI and a JSON API under
\fI/api\fR. \fBPOST /api/login\fR \fB{Username, Password, TOTP}\fR sets the
session cookie and an \fIovcp_csrf\fR cookie; every mutating request echoes
the latter in an \fBX\-OVCP\-CSRF\fR header. Each route below calls the same
implementation as its CLI twin, with the same inputs, checks and audit
line. Settings and upstream routes return the same object as the twin's
\fB\-json\fR output, plus a \fBwarning\fR string where the CLI prints
\fBwarning:\fR to stderr; actions return \fB{"ok": true}\fR or their result.
Errors are \fB{"error": "..."}\fR with a 4xx/5xx status. Roles nest: \fBoperator\fR may do what
\fBreadonly\fR may, \fBadmin\fR everything.
.TS
l l l.
Route	Role	CLI twin
_
GET /healthz	none	\(em (liveness: database ping)
GET /api/me	readonly	\(em (user, role, server CN)
GET /api/status	readonly	status
GET /api/stats	readonly	stats
GET /api/audit	readonly	audit
GET /api/certs	readonly	certificate list
