.PP
CLI only, by design: \fBinit\fR, \fBserve\fR, \fBca\fR (a CA mistake
affects every certificate), \fBbackup restore\fR (offline recovery),
\fBcertificate renew \-cn\fR (a rename), \fBstats \-follow\fR,
\fBcompletion\fR and \fBversion\fR.
.SH TIMESTAMPS
.TP
.B CLI
Always the server's own local zone. \fBovcp\fR is local\-only \(en it reads
\fB$OVCP_DATA\fR and dials local Unix sockets directly, never a remote
instance \(en so "the CLI's zone" and "the server's zone" are the same
thing by construction.
.TP
.B openvpn.log / dnsmasq.log
RFC 3339, one explicit UTC offset per line, generated by \fBovcp\fR itself
(neither worker is trusted to timestamp its own output \(en see
\fBSELINUX\fR for why dnsmasq's own stamp went away). Self\-describing:
correct however the raw file is later read, wherever that is.
.TP
.B ovcp.log
Same local zone, via Go's \fBslog\fR default \fBtime=\fR field.
.TP
.B Backup/audit archive filenames
Server's local zone, no offset in the string (colons break filenames) \(en
never re\-parsed, so no ambiguity in practice.
.TP
.B Web UI
The \fBadmin's own browser/OS zone\fR, not the server's. Every timestamp is
parsed once into an absolute instant, then rendered with local getters \(en
an admin in Tokyo viewing a Frankfurt server sees Tokyo time, correctly.
.PP
No code path mixes in a VPN client's own timezone anywhere \(en OpenVPN
connections carry no timezone information, and nothing here needs them to.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B OVCP_DATA
Data directory \(en the source of truth for where ovcp keeps everything
(see \fBFILES\fR). Defaults to \fI/var/lib/ovcp\fR if unset; \fB\-data\fR
overrides it for a single invocation.
.TP
.B OVCP_LISTEN
Admin UI listen address(es), comma\-separated. Each gets the full
HTTPS/auth stack. VPN\-side addresses (e.g. 10.8.0.1:8443, reachable by
connected clients) may be bound before the tun interface exists
(IP_FREEBIND).
.TP
.B OVCP_MGMT_SOCK
OpenVPN management socket (default \fI/run/ovcp/mgmt.sock\fR);
\fBserve \-mgmt\fR sets it for a single invocation. Only \fBserve\fR
connects to it.
.TP
.B OVCP_CTRL_SOCK
Control socket through which CLI commands reach a running \fBserve\fR
(default \fI/run/ovcp/control.sock\fR); \fBserve \-ctrl\fR sets it for a
single invocation. Independent of \fBOVCP_MGMT_SOCK\fR: set both to move
all of \fBserve\fR's sockets out of \fI/run/ovcp\fR. Internals:
\fBARCHITECTURE.md\fR.
.TP
.B OVCP_STATUS_INTERVAL
Seconds between openvpn rewriting \fI/run/ovcp/status\fR (see \fBFILES\fR)
and how often the web UI polls it (default \fB3\fR, clamped to 1\-60).
Invalid values fall back to the default with a logged warning.
.TP
.B OVCP_SERVER_CN
Default client \fBremote\fR for \fBcertificate issue \-export\fR and CN for the
admin UI certificate (falls back to the VPN server certificate's CN).
.TP
.B OVCP_CA_PASSPHRASE, OVCP_USER_PASSWORD
Non\-interactive passphrase/password for automation, tests, and container
init (the image defaults both to \fIchangeme!\fR). Avoid on
shared systems: environment variables can leak.
.TP
.B OVCP_CA_NEW_PASSPHRASE, OVCP_BACKUP_PASSPHRASE
Non\-interactive equivalents for \fBca rotate\fR's new passphrase and
\fBbackup\fR's archive passphrase. Same leak caveat as above.
.TP
.B OVCP_YOLO_PASSWORD
Any non\-empty value waives the strength check on every passphrase and
password above (see \fBSECURITY\fR).
.TP
.B OVCP_TELEGRAM_TOKEN
Non\-interactive equivalent for \fBtelegram config set \-token\fR's bot
token. Same leak caveat as above; stored in the database afterward
regardless (see \fBSECURITY\fR).
.TP
.B OVCP_TELEGRAM_ADMIN
Non\-interactive equivalent for \fBtelegram config set\fR's \fB\-admin\fR
flag: the numeric Telegram id or \fB@username\fR the bot will ever respond
to.
.TP
.B OVCP_USER
Account a root invocation drops to (default \fIovcp\fR; see
\fBPRIVILEGES\fR). \fBroot\fR opts out (containers or user namespaces
where root is already confined).
.TP
.B OVCP_OPENVPN_PROTO, OVCP_OPENVPN_PORT, OVCP_OPENVPN_SUBNET,
.B OVCP_OPENVPN_CIPHER, OVCP_OPENVPN_DNS, OVCP_OPENVPN_ROUTES,
.B OVCP_OPENVPN_REDIRECT_GW, OVCP_OPENVPN_EXTRA
Seed every openvpn server setting \fBvpn config get\fR/\fBvpn config set\fR/
the web UI's Settings tab can show or change \(en \fIudp\fR, \fI1194\fR,
\fI10.8.0.0/24\fR, \fIAES\-256\-GCM\fR, unset (no pushed DNS), unset (no
pushed routes), \fItrue\fR (set \fBOVCP_OPENVPN_REDIRECT_GW=false\fR to
disable), and empty by default, respectively. \fBOVCP_OPENVPN_DNS\fR and
\fBOVCP_OPENVPN_ROUTES\fR take a comma\-separated list, same as \fBvpn
config set\fR's \fB\-dns\fR/\fB\-routes\fR flags. There is no env var for
\fB\-push\-internal\-dns\fR \(en it defaults to off, except that \fBinit\fR
itself turns it on when it finds dnsmasq on \fBPATH\fR.
.TP
.B OVCP_DNS_BLOCK_INTERNET
Seeds \fBdns config set \-block\-internet\fR: \fItrue\fR starts with it on.
.TP
.B OVCP_UPSTREAMS_ENABLED
Whether the
.B upstream
feature (see \fBCOMMANDS\fR) is on: default \fBtrue\fR, but only takes
effect when nftables is also found on \fBPATH\fR \(en set to \fBfalse\fR to
turn it off even when nftables is present. Same
\(lqanything but \fBfalse\fR means on\(rq rule as \fBOVCP_LOG_ROTATE_ENABLED\fR.
.TP
.B OVCP_UPSTREAM_SOCKS
Seeds one SOCKS upstream (id \fBsocks\fR, set as the default) from a
.I socks5://[user:pass@]host:port
URL (see \fBupstream add \-socks\fR). Invalid or already superseded by a
saved config: ignored, logged, never fatal.
.TP
.B OVCP_UPSTREAM_SOCKS_DIAL_TIMEOUT, OVCP_UPSTREAM_SOCKS_HANDSHAKE_TIMEOUT
Seconds; server\-wide default for the SOCKS relay's connect timeout
(default \fB10\fR) and SOCKS negotiation timeout (default \fB30\fR) \(en
the handshake bound is separate and more generous by default since a Tor
backend's real circuit\-build time can run past a plain TCP connect's. Any
upstream's own \fB\-dial\-timeout\fR/\fB\-handshake\-timeout\fR (see
\fBupstream add\fR in \fBCOMMANDS\fR) overrides these per\-upstream, e.g. a
low value for a fast local backend alongside a high one for Tor in the
same config. Invalid or non\-positive values fall back to the default. No
\fIiface\fR/
\fIgateway\fR equivalent exists: that mode is plain kernel routing (\fBip
route\fR/\fBip rule\fR), not a software connection with anything to time
out \(en its kill\-switch is an unreachable fallback route, not time\-based.
.TP
.B OVCP_STATS_SAMPLE_INTERVAL, OVCP_STATS_RETENTION_HOURS
Seed \fBstats config set\fR's \fB\-sample\-interval\-sec\fR/
\fB\-retention\-hours\fR (default \fB60\fR seconds, \fB24\fR hours).
.TP
.B OVCP_LOG_ROTATE_ENABLED, OVCP_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS, OVCP_LOG_KEEP_UNCOMPRESSED
Seed \fBlogs config set\fR's \fB\-enabled\fR/\fB\-retention\-days\fR/
\fB\-keep\-uncompressed\fR (default enabled, \fB14\fR days, \fB2\fR
generations kept uncompressed); for \fBOVCP_LOG_ROTATE_ENABLED\fR, anything
but \fBfalse\fR means on.
.TP
.B OVCP_OPENVPN_AUTOSTART, OVCP_DNSMASQ_AUTOSTART
Set to \fBfalse\fR to skip starting that worker automatically when
\fBserve\fR starts (default: both start). Either can still be started
manually afterward (\fBovcp vpn start\fR / \fBovcp dns start\fR, or the
web UI); this only gates the boot\-time launch.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Same as \fB\-no\-color\fR.
.TP
.B OVCP_JSON
Same as \fB\-json\fR.
.SH FILES
All paths below are rooted at \fB$OVCP_DATA\fR (default \fI/var/lib/ovcp\fR)
\(en set it once and everything follows, including these.
.TP
.I $OVCP_DATA/pki/ca.crt, ca.key.enc
CA certificate and encrypted private key.
.TP
.I $OVCP_DATA/pki/server.crt, server.key
Server keypair used by openvpn. Not included in \fBbackup\fR archives \(en
reissued by \fBcertificate renew\fR after a restore.
.TP
.I $OVCP_DATA/pki/crl.pem
Certificate revocation list (referenced by \fBcrl\-verify\fR).
.TP
.I $OVCP_DATA/pki/tls\-crypt.key
OpenVPN static key for tls\-crypt (control channel encryption).
.TP
.I $OVCP_DATA/totp.key
Machine\-local key that decrypts each user's TOTP secret in the database.
Unlike the CA key, not passphrase\-protected \(en ovcp must read it
unattended on every restart. Included in \fBbackup\fR archives.
.TP
.I $OVCP_DATA/server.conf
Generated openvpn configuration. Managed by ovcp; do not edit.
.TP
.I $OVCP_DATA/ovcp.db
SQLite database: users, sessions, certificate metadata, audit log,
connection stats (retention: \fBstats config\fR). Private keys are never
stored in the database.
.TP
.I $OVCP_DATA/admin\-tls/
Self\-signed certificate for the admin UI, issued by its own key, never by
\fBpki/ca.key.enc\fR. That CA's only job is deciding which client
certs get VPN access; sharing it with the browser\-facing cert would let
a CA compromise MITM the web login too, not just mint VPN clients. Its
CN is a snapshot taken at \fBovcp serve\fR startup \(en a
\fBcertificate renew \-cn\fR rename needs \fBserve\fR restarted to
regenerate it, unlike everything else that reads the server CN.
.TP
.I $OVCP_DATA/logs/openvpn.log
openvpn's stdout/stderr, appended across restarts, each line prefixed with
an RFC3339 timestamp \fBovcp\fR itself adds (openvpn's own is suppressed
\(en see \fBsuppress\-timestamps\fR in \fIserver.conf\fR). Rotated
internally by default \(en see \fBlogs\fR in \fBCOMMANDS\fR.
.TP
.I $OVCP_DATA/logs/ovcp.log
ovcp's own log, mirrored to stderr/journal. \fBkey=value\fR text by
default, or one JSON object per line with \fB\-json\fR (see \fBOPTIONS\fR).
Rotated the same way as \fIopenvpn.log\fR.
.TP
.I $OVCP_DATA/logs/dnsmasq.log
dnsmasq's stdout/stderr, appended across restarts, RFC3339\-stamped the
same way as \fIopenvpn.log\fR \(en dnsmasq never opens this path itself
(see \fBSELINUX\fR). Rotated the same way.
.TP
.I $OVCP_DATA/logs/*.log\-\fIYYYYMMDDThhmmssZ\fR[.gz]
Rotated generations (see \fBlogs\fR): plain text for the newest few, gzip
beyond that, deleted past the configured retention.
.TP
.I $OVCP_DATA/dns/certs.hosts
One line per connected, included certificate: server always, clients
while they're online. Regenerated automatically; do not edit.
.TP
.I $OVCP_DATA/dns/custom.hosts
Manually added hosts\-file entries, edited via \fBrecord edit\fR or the
Records tab. Not touched by ovcp otherwise.
.TP
.I /var/log/ovcp
Symlink to \fI$OVCP_DATA/logs\fR (package/container installs), for a
log\-shipper or an external \fBlogrotate\fR used instead of the internal one.
.TP
.I $OVCP_MGMT_SOCK, $OVCP_CTRL_SOCK
The OpenVPN management socket (unix only, never TCP) and ovcp's
owner\-only (0600) control socket; defaults in \fBENVIRONMENT\fR.
.TP
.I /run/ovcp/status
openvpn's periodic connected\-clients snapshot, read by \fBstatus\fR/
\fBstats \-follow\fR/the web UI instead of polling the mgmt socket (which
would log a line per poll). Live state, not a log: excluded from
\fBbackup\fR and from log rotation.
.SH EXAMPLES
Issue and download a profile for a phone, valid two years:
.nf
.RS
ovcp certificate issue \-cn dana\-phone \-days 730 \-export \-remote vpn.example.com > dana\-phone.ovpn
.RE
.fi
Revoke it later:
.nf
.RS
ovcp certificate list
ovcp certificate revoke \-serial 682b2da83f4d...
.RE
.fi
Renew that same client's profile before it expires, then renew the openvpn
server cert and the CA certificate itself (both admin, both need a restart
to take effect):
.nf
.RS
(umask 077; ovcp certificate issue \-cn dana\-phone \-renew \-export > dana\-phone.ovpn)
ovcp certificate renew
ovcp ca renew
ovcp vpn restart
.RE
.fi
Serve the admin UI on the host loopback and again inside the VPN itself, so
it's reachable through the tunnel without ever exposing it publicly (works
even before the tunnel interface exists \(en see \fBIP_FREEBIND\fR under \fBOVCP_LISTEN\fR):
.nf
.RS
OVCP_LISTEN=127.0.0.1:8443,10.8.0.1:8443 ovcp serve
.RE
.fi
A laptop that should reach the VPN subnet but keep its own default route
(the server redirects all traffic for everyone else):
.nf
.RS
ovcp certificate issue \-cn dana\-laptop \-export \-remote vpn.example.com \-split\-tunnel > dana\-laptop.ovpn
.RE
.fi
Add a couple of extra directives to one client's profile, and separately
edit the server's own extra directives:
.nf
.RS
ovcp certificate issue \-cn dana\-phone \-export \-remote vpn.example.com \e
  \-custom\-opts block\-outside\-dns \-custom\-opts "ping\-restart 30" > dana\-phone.ovpn
ovcp vpn config set \-extra \-
.RE
.fi
Move the server to TCP/443 and stop routing all client traffic through
the VPN, previewing the \fIserver.conf\fR change first:
.nf
.RS
ovcp vpn config preview \-proto tcp \-port 443 \-redirect\-gw off
ovcp vpn config set \-proto tcp \-port 443 \-redirect\-gw off
ovcp vpn restart
.RE
.fi
Push the internal dnsmasq resolver to clients and block dnsmasq from
forwarding anywhere else \(en internal cert\-hostnames still resolve,
nothing else does:
.nf
.RS
ovcp vpn config set \-push\-internal\-dns on
ovcp dns config set \-block\-internet on
ovcp vpn restart && ovcp dns restart
.RE
.fi
Non\-attended container init with a non\-default subnet and DNS, no CLI
flags or browser involved:
.nf
.RS
OVCP_OPENVPN_SUBNET=10.20.0.0/24 OVCP_OPENVPN_DNS=1.1.1.1,9.9.9.9 \e
  ovcp init \-server\-cn vpn.example.com
.RE
.fi
Scripting: list expired CNs without parsing column output (needs \fBjq\fR):
.nf
.RS
ovcp \-json certificate list | jq \-r '.[] | select(.status == "expired") | .cn'
.RE
.fi
Watch one client's live bandwidth while troubleshooting a slow connection:
.nf
.RS
ovcp stats \-cn dana\-phone \-follow
.RE
.fi
.SH PRIVILEGES
Started as root \(en any command, not just \fBserve\fR \(en \fBovcp\fR
becomes \fBOVCP_USER\fR (default \fIovcp\fR) in place: same process, no
root process left, keeping only \fBCAP_NET_ADMIN\fR and
\fBCAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE\fR (those it holds), which openvpn, dnsmasq and nft
inherit. Root at most creates \fI$OVCP_DATA\fR itself and never reads or
writes anything inside it. Under systemd the unit
does this itself (\fBUser=\fR, \fBAmbientCapabilities=\fR) and ovcp never
sees root.
.PP
\fBovcp\fR sets an owner only on a \fI$OVCP_DATA\fR or \fI/run/ovcp\fR it
creates itself; it never re\-owns an existing path, so a pre\-existing one
must already belong to that account (packages, the container image and the
unit's \fBStateDirectory=\fR arrange this). The account must be able to
reach both the binary and \fI$OVCP_DATA\fR, so neither can be inside a
private \fI$HOME\fR. Every directory is \fI0700\fR; files range from \fI0600\fR (private keys,
\fIovcp.db\fR) to \fI0644\fR (the CA/server certificates and the CRL,
meant to be publicly verifiable); \fBinit \-skeleton\fR re\-applies the
modes. \fI/run/ovcp\fR holds the management and control sockets.
Containers need \fB\-\-cap\-add NET_ADMIN\fR.
.PP
openvpn and dnsmasq (optional, used if found on \fIPATH\fR) run as
foreground children of ovcp and cannot outlive it (Pdeathsig); dnsmasq
reads its own \fI$OVCP_DATA/dns\fR via \fB\-\-hostsdir\fR.
.PP
The admin UI and openvpn supervision run in the same process at the same
privilege level; \fBARCHITECTURE.md\fR gives the rationale. The systemd
unit additionally sandboxes the process tree (\fBProtectSystem=strict\fR,
\fBNoNewPrivileges\fR, \fBMemoryDenyWriteExecute\fR and more).
.SH DEPLOYMENT
One runtime, everywhere: ovcp owns the openvpn worker (fork/exec + signals,
no SIGHUP, no in\-place reload) the same way on bare metal, under systemd,
and inside a container. If ovcp restarts, its \fBserve\fR process brings
openvpn back up.
.SS systemd
A single unit runs ovcp unprivileged (see \fBPRIVILEGES\fR); ovcp starts
and stops openvpn itself.
Distro packages install \fIovcp.service\fR automatically; a from\-source
install (\fBBUILD.md\fR, binary and account) needs it copied in manually:
.nf
.RS
sudo install \-m644 deploy/systemd/ovcp.service /usr/lib/systemd/system/
sudo ovcp init \-server\-cn vpn.example.com
sudo systemctl enable \-\-now ovcp
.RE
.fi
Overriding \fBOVCP_DATA\fR (e.g. via \fBsystemctl edit ovcp\fR) also needs
a matching \fBStateDirectory=\fR \(en \fBProtectSystem=strict\fR
silently blocks writes anywhere else.
.SS container
One all\-in\-one image (\fBmake image\fR): ovcp drops to the image's \fIovcp\fR
account and starts openvpn inside the container. Init once, then run:
.nf
.RS
podman run \-\-rm \-v ovcp:/var/lib/ovcp ovcp init \-server\-cn vpn.example.com
podman run \-d \-\-cap\-add=NET_ADMIN \-\-device /dev/net/tun \e
  \-p 1194:1194/udp \-p 127.0.0.1:8443:8443 \-v ovcp:/var/lib/ovcp ovcp
.RE
.fi
The image defaults \fBOVCP_CA_PASSPHRASE\fR/\fBOVCP_USER_PASSWORD\fR to
\fIchangeme!\fR for the one\-shot init \(en override with \fB\-e\fR. To split
the UI and the VPN across network interfaces, bind each published port to
the right address, e.g.
\fB\-p 203.0.113.7:1194:1194/udp \-p 192.168.1.10:8443:8443\fR. docker works
the same; the Containerfile is plain OCI.
.PP
Client \fBRealAddress\fR (openvpn's own \fBstatus 3\fR/CLIENT_LIST, not
ovcp's HTTP layer) can come back as one container\-internal address for
every client instead of distinct public IPs. Root cause, on any runtime:
a published UDP port routed through a userspace proxy instead of
kernel\-level DNAT replaces the real source address with the proxy's own.
Which runtime does this varies:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDocker\fR: \fBdocker\-proxy\fR (userland\-proxy), on by default. Fix:
run the daemon with \fB\-\-userland\-proxy=false\fR (check via
\fBdocker info | grep \-i userland\fR) \(en pure kernel DNAT, no loss of
network isolation (unlike \fBnetwork_mode: host\fR, not recommended here).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBPodman\fR: rootful is already kernel DNAT (netavark), unaffected.
Rootless defaults to \fBpasta\fR since Podman 5.0, which also preserves
the source address \(en only the older \fBslirp4netns\fR backend (still
selectable, or the default pre\-5.0) has this problem. Fix: drop
\fB\-\-network slirp4netns\fR (let it use the pasta default), or run
rootful.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBKubernetes\fR: kube\-proxy's default \fBCluster\fR traffic policy SNATs
external traffic. Fix: set \fBservice.spec.externalTrafficPolicy: Local\fR.
.RE
.PP
\fBGET /healthz\fR is an unauthenticated, database\-ping health check \(en
point a container/Kubernetes liveness or readiness probe at it.
.SH NETWORKING
Three independent listeners, three independent bind rules.
.TP
.B Admin UI
\fBOVCP_LISTEN\fR (comma\-separated addresses, see \fBENVIRONMENT\fR) \(en
the only one of the three with a first\-class setting. Defaults to
\fI127.0.0.1:8443\fR (loopback only).
.TP
.B openvpn
No bind\-address setting exists; the rendered \fIserver.conf\fR never emits
a \fBlocal\fR directive, so openvpn listens on \fBall interfaces\fR on its
configured \fBport\fR/\fBproto\fR. To scope it to one address, add
\fBlocal <IP>\fR yourself via \fBvpn config set \-extra\fR (see \fBvpn
config\fR in \fBCOMMANDS\fR); there is no dedicated flag.
.TP
.B dnsmasq
Not configurable: \fB\-\-listen\-address\fR is always
the VPN's own tunnel IP (the server's \fBServerTunIP()\fR, e.g.
\fI10.8.0.1\fR). DNS only ever answers already\-connected VPN clients; it
has no reason to exist on any other address, so there is no setting for one.
.PP
\fBSame address for the admin UI and the VPN\fR (the common single\-NIC
case, and the default): set \fBOVCP_LISTEN\fR to that address (or leave it
on loopback if the UI is only ever reached over the tunnel/SSH) and take
openvpn's all\-interfaces default as is. Nothing else to configure.
.PP
\fBSeparate, so a public VPN client can't reach the admin UI at all\fR:
.RS
.nf
OVCP_LISTEN=127.0.0.1:8443,10.8.0.1:8443
.fi
.RE
admin UI only on loopback and the VPN's own tunnel address, while openvpn
keeps its public\-facing port. A client on the internet can reach the VPN
port to connect, but can't reach the admin UI until they're already
tunneled in (or have local shell access). Add \fBlocal <public\-ip>\fR via
\fB\-extra\fR too if openvpn itself should also stop listening on other
interfaces. See \fBDEPLOYMENT\fR's container section for the equivalent
port\-mapping form of this same split.
.PP
\fBBaseline routing\fR: a client reaching the internet over the box's own
default route needs IPv4 forwarding, which ovcp never sets, and outbound
NAT. With \fBnft\fR(8) on \fBPATH\fR (the \fBupstream\fR feature enabled),
ovcp NATs the tunnel itself: its own table masquerades traffic arriving
on \fBovcp0\fR, installed on every \fBvpn start\fR and removed with the
table. Without nft, NAT is yours too:
.RS
.nf
sysctl \-w net.ipv4.ip_forward=1
iptables \-t nat \-A POSTROUTING \-o eth0 \-j MASQUERADE    # only without nft
.fi
.RE
.B status
and
.B vpn start
check what ovcp doesn't provide and print one
.B warning:
line if it looks off; \fBserve\fR logs it at start and the web UI's
dashboard shows it live \(en advisory, never fatal. Without nft the NAT
check reads the iptables
.B nat
table; NAT done elsewhere (firewalld, a router upstream) is reported as
unverifiable, not missing.
.SH SELINUX
On SELinux systems the confined
.BR openvpn (8)
and
.BR dnsmasq (8)
domains cannot read a non\-standard data directory. deb/rpm packages run
\fIdeploy/selinux.sh\fR automatically on install (labels the data dir
\fIopenvpn_etc_t\fR, \fB$OVCP_DATA/dns\fR \fIdnsmasq_etc_t\fR, and the
logs \fIopenvpn_var_log_t\fR; honors \fB$OVCP_DATA\fR; a no\-op if the
box isn't confined, never fails the install). Building from source or
installing the raw binary: run \fIdeploy/selinux.sh\fR once yourself.
ovcp itself runs unconfined and needs no policy; neither worker's log
file needs a label either \(en both are opened by ovcp itself, never
by the confined child.
